The kernel used in the C5 series is compiled to only support binaries compiled with `EF_MIPS_NAN2008`. This is a problem as very few packages with this flag exist outside of building them yourself.
To remediate this, we patch a magic offset in memory every boot to allow the kernel to run these binaries. (specifically [this field](<https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/59dee6d28756c629f3a0bb56266f80e36ef7c99c/arch/mips/kernel/elf.c#L164>))
**This means it depends on your kernel package version, so please only apply this if you have a matching kernel package version.**
To check your kernel package version, please do this:
`ls /usr/prog/PROGRAM/kernel/`
Take the highest version number and map it to the offset here:
```
2.0.1: 0x00a130d1
```
Next, run `busybox devmem <offset> 8` (this does not write anything). It should be `0x00`
If all is good, you can add this to the top of `/usr/prog/app_startup.sh`, on a line right after the shebang (`#!`):
```
# allow legacy nan binaries to run
busybox devmem <offset> 8 1
```
This technically allows non-standard behavior with how NaN is handled on legacy nan binaries, but the vast majority of binaries won't be affected.
